    "suppliedAnswer": "(1) Producer optimisation: the relative goods price equals the marginal rate of transformation, Px/Py = MRT, so output value is maximised on the PPF. (2) Consumer optimisation: the relative goods price equals the marginal rate of substitution, Px/Py = MRS, so the consumer chooses the best affordable basket. (3) Market clearing: production equals consumption for each good, Xp = Xc and Yp = Yc. Together these conditions locate the autarky equilibrium. 3.2 General Equilibrium in an Open (Trading) Economy With trade, relative world prices p* = Px*/Py* generally differ from autarky prices. The optimisation rules remain: producers use MRT = p* and consumers use MRS = p*. The crucial replacement is the balance-of-trade equation Px*Xp + Py*Yp = Px*Xc + Py*Yc. A country can import more of one good than it produces if exports of the other good pay for it. The consumption point may therefore lie beyond its PPF even while its production point remains on the frontier. Producers optimize by equating the marginal rate of transformation to whatever prices prevail, and, similarly, consumers optimize by equating their marginal rate of substitution to those prices. With international trade, an economy is no longer constrained to consume only what it can produce. A trading economy is able to sell some of one good at world prices and use the proceeds to buy the other commodity. If excess demand is negative, an economy is consuming less than it is producing, resulting in an export good. An alternative way of saying this is that the sum of the value of the country’s excess demands must equal zero: the positive excess demand for the import good must equal the negative excess demand for the export good.",
