    "questionText": "Discuss specialisation and exchange as features of modern international trade.",
    "suppliedAnswer": "Modern trade involves countries, firms and regions concentrating production in goods for which their resource use and opportunity costs are relatively favourable, then exchanging output to satisfy varied consumption needs. Specialisation can increase productive efficiency or exploit scale; exchange makes imported goods available at terms preferable to domestic production. These processes link national markets, but the results depend on prices, technology, resource endowments, demand and policy barriers. 3.3 Distribution of Gains with Heterogeneous Tastes People differ in preferences; they may value combinations of X and Y differently, even when they face the same price ratio. This changes how much each person benefits from access to foreign trade. The analysis uses community indifference-curve reasoning to show that aggregate gains do not imply every individual enjoys an identical improvement. Distribution must therefore be studied separately from the existence of total gains. Consider consumers who have equal incomes but different tastes for two goods. At the same world price ratio, one may gain substantially by being able to buy more of an imported good, while another may choose a basket close to the pre-trade bundle. A common market price does not imply common welfare gains because individual indifference curves differ. The economy can show an aggregate gain while the allocation of those benefits is uneven. Consequently, a proper evaluation distinguishes the total increase in purchasing opportunities froin the personal gains -- 17 of 38 --",
