    "questionText": "List five conditions that can guarantee a no-trade situation.",
    "suppliedAnswer": "(1) Countries possess the same relative factor endowments; (2) they have identical technologies for producing each good; (3) production shows constant returns to scale; (4) tastes or preferences are identical and homogeneous; and (5) there are no distortions such as taxes, subsidies or imperfect competition. Together these conditions can yield identical relative autarky prices so that no gain arises from exchanging the two goods. 3.2 Methodological Considerations Trade models deliberately simplify complex economies to identify causal relationships. Two-good and two-factor assumptions permit clear analysis of relative prices, resource allocation and gains. Results need qualification when transport costs, taxes, imperfect competition, unequal ownership of factors, varying tastes and factor mobility are admitted. The comparison of autarky and trade should distinguish positive analysis (what occurs) from welfare assessment (whether society or individual groups benefit). To assess the relevance of this criticism, we must understand why this approach is being employed and what kinds of conclusions we expect to draw froin the analysis. It is clear that no conclusions about a specific cause of trade can be derived unless we can be sure that no other things are causing trade at the time. For example, we could not identify the effects of demand differences in a model in which endowments were also different, for it would generally be impossible to separate the effects of these two variables. At this stage of the analysis, then, the question of whether the model is “realistic” is not a relevant one, for no claim has been made about its predictive powers. 3.3 Consequences or Effects of International Trade Major concerns include problems for developing countries: dual economies where export-oriented modern sectors coexist with neglected traditional ones; situations in which poorer nations capture limited benefits; possible deterioration in terms of trade for primary products; and demonstration effects where imported consumption standards increase demand for luxury items. Changes in global demand, substitution of synthetic products for agricultural goods and foreign protection of domestic producers can further constrain export incomes. Positive effects include access to goods, technology and larger markets, but distribution and resilience matter. The effects of foreign factor movements have been that of creating a highly unbalanced structure of production of these countries. Although export increased but they did not contribute much to the development of the rest of the economy. During depression, terms of trade become adverse and their foreign exchange earnings fall steeply. It has been maintained that the inflow of foreign capital and developed a country’s natural resources only for export purposes, to the neglect of production in the domestic sector. Singer is also of the opinion that the benefits of technological progress have gone disproportionately to the advanced countries. -- 19 of 38 --",
