    "questionText": "Differentiate residual-claimant and bargaining theories of wages.",
    "suppliedAnswer": "Residual-claimant theory treats wages as the portion of product value left after land, capital and other claimants receive their rewards. It therefore makes the wage bill a residual in the distribution process. Bargaining theory states that actual wages, working hours and employment conditions are determined through negotiation and the relative bargaining power of employees and employers. The first is chiefly a theory of distributive shares; the second stresses bargaining institutions and strength. 3.2 The Role of Wages When productivity differs between countries, nominal wage differences do not determine comparative advantage by themselves. In competitive equilibrium, a firm can pay higher wages if each worker produces more output. The relevant international cost of a good is the wage rate multiplied by unit labour requirements. Comparing those costs across industries determines which country can supply each good competitively. Trade can change the purchasing power of wages as workers use income to buy domestic and imported goods. 3.3 Model of Wages in the Ricardian Framework Let wH and wF be wages in Home and Foreign, and aHX and aFX the respective unit labour requirements in commodity X. Unit labour costs are wH aHX and wF aFX. Home can supply X at lower cost if wH aHX < wF aFX. Similarly compare costs for Y. The model connects productivity with international wage gaps and shows why an absolute productivity advantage can correspond to a higher real wage while the direction of trade follows comparative cost differences. Relative prices must equal relative real costs in terms of labor inputs. Here, if sector Y has a relatively low marginal product and sector X has a relatively high marginal product, the home country is likely to have its comparative advantage in good X. The wage rate, y which is the nominal price of labor, has no effect on relative commodity prices as long as it is the same in both sectors. The wage rate is relevant for determining real wages, or the living standards of laborers. This change will alter nominal wages in both countries, generating wage rates Wh and Wf.",
