    "questionText": "Explain the Rybczynski theorem.",
    "suppliedAnswer": "At constant commodity prices, an increase in the supply of one factor increases production of the commodity that uses it relatively intensively and reduces production of the other commodity, as long as both sectors operate. If labour expands, the labour-intensive sector grows and the capital-intensive sector contracts. Its logic rests on factor reallocation at unchanged prices, and the resulting changes can be magnified relative to the original increase in labour. 3.3 Trade Theory with Many Goods and Factors Moving from two goods and two factors to many products complicates precise commodity-by-commodity predictions. In this setting, rankings by factor intensity no longer always yield a unique trade pattern. Nevertheless, a country rich in a factor is generally expected to export a bundle of commodities embodying relatively more of that factor. The reasoning shifts from an individual exported commodity to the aggregate factor content of the entire trade bundle. When products are numerous, factor intensities form more than one simple ranking. A country might export some relatively labour-intensive products and also some sophisticated capital-intensive goods, depending on their specific costs and the wider production structure. It can still be useful to compare total factor content of all exports with total factor content of all imports. The many-goods extension therefore makes a narrower prediction about individual commodities but retains the basic link between endowments and the factor composition of a country’s trade bundle. -- 28 of 38 --",
