    "suppliedAnswer": "A commodity price is the money or relative price paid for an output good, such as X or Y. A factor price is remuneration for an input used in production, such as the wage for labour or rental for capital. Product prices affect the value of marginal productivity; in a competitive sector, employment decisions are related by w = P times marginal product of labour. A rise in an export good’s price can therefore shift wages and the earnings of industry-specific factors. 3.4 Exchange Rate Policies The main categories are freely floating exchange rates (market demand and supply largely determine the rate), managed floats (authorities intervene while permitting movements) and fixed or pegged systems (the rate is officially linked to an anchor). Flexible rates permit adjustment to economic changes but entail currency risk; fixed rates improve rate predictability but may require substantial reserve and policy commitments. Exchange-rate policy affects the domestic price of traded commodities, import bills and export receipts. The unit also lists payment arrangements used in international trade. Under a floating system, market forces are permitted to alter currency prices when demand and supply change. A managed float allows official intervention to limit or guide movements. A fixed-rate system promises stability against an anchor but may require reserves or direct controls when market pressures increase. For exporters, exchange-rate depreciation can raise domestic-currency receipts from foreign sales; for importers, it can make overseas purchases and foreign-currency debts more costly. These effects depend on prices and contracts, so the distinction between short-term adjustment and longer-term production responses is important. -- 31 of 38 --",
