    "questionText": "Summarise factor-price equalisation and its limitations.",
    "suppliedAnswer": "Trade can bring commodity prices closer together, which in the standard diversified, common-technology model tends to equalise the returns to labour and capital across countries. That result requires strong assumptions about production and competition. Where specific factors, different technologies, trade barriers or incomplete specialisation operate, equal prices for traded goods need not imply identical wages and returns to capital. 3.3 Endowment Changes and Outputs Changing the supply of one factor shifts the economy’s output possibilities toward activities that use the expanding resource more heavily. At fixed product prices in a standard Heckscher-Ohlin setting, the Rybczynski effect predicts expansion of the intensive sector and contraction of the other sector. In the specific-factors setting, effects differ because some productive resources cannot leave their sectors quickly. The model chosen determines the precise direction of adjustment. Again, this is due to the mobility of labor rather than to the intensity of production. Factor endowments and outputs An increase in one specific factor increases the output of the commodity that uses that factor and reduces the output of the other industry. Increases in the supply of the mobile factor will expand both outputs. It is clear from our discussion that mobile factors would oppose policies allowing freer immigration of competing mobile factors, while owners of specific factors would favor them. Again, this is a result of factor mobility rather than factor scarcity, as in the Heckscher-Ohlin model. 3.4 The Pattern of Trade In the specific-factors model, trade patterns depend on the ownership and size of sector-specific resources as well as relative labour availability. An economy may export goods using its relatively large specific-factor endowment. Endowment growth can alter comparative costs and hence future export composition. The analysis points out that factor-specific circumstances are especially relevant to short-run trade and policy debates. A larger capital stock or a bigger workforce changes the potential scale and composition of production. If the extra capital belongs to export-oriented manufacturing, output in that sector may expand and change the country’s comparative costs. In another case, growth in labour supply can alter relative factor returns and shift production toward more labour-using activities. Relative world prices also matter: a change in domestic supplies produces a different market response when the country is too small to influence world prices than when it can alter its terms of trade. -- 33 of 38 --",
