    "suppliedAnswer": "1. Price-Wedge Formulation:\n- A commodity consumption tax inserts a wedge between consumer prices (q) and producer prices (p): q = p(1 + tc). Consumers face higher prices while producers receive the world or base price.\n- A production tax or subsidy inserts a wedge between producer receipts and consumer prices: pp = p(1 - tp) or pp = p(1 + sp), while consumer prices remain at market equilibrium.\n\n2. Equivalence of an Import Tariff:\n- An import tariff of rate t on imported good X raises the domestic internal price of X for both domestic producers and domestic consumers above the world price P*x: Pd_x = P*x(1 + t).\n- For domestic producers, it acts identically to a production subsidy of rate t, incentivizing domestic import-competing firms to expand output from X1 to X2, generating a production distortion loss (triangle a).\n- For domestic consumers, it acts identically to an excise consumption tax of rate t, driving consumption down from C1 to C2, generating a consumption deadweight loss (triangle b).\n- Therefore, a tariff creates dual economic distortions (production inefficiency and consumer welfare loss) equal to the sum of a production subsidy plus a consumption tax on the traded commodity.",
