    "questionText": "Explain how an inter-industry wage differential distortion (wx > wy) disrupts production efficiency and alters the Gains-from-Trade theorem.",
    "suppliedAnswer": "1. Mechanism of Factor Market Distortion (Wage Differential):\n- In a distortion-free economy, competitive factor mobility equalizes wages across sectors: wx = wy = w.\n- When a distortion arises (e.g., minimum wage law in the manufacturing sector X, trade union wage markup, or urban-rural wage gap), wx = (1 + μ)wy, where μ > 0.\n\n2. Failure of Production Efficiency:\n- Efficiency requires equality of marginal rates of technical substitution (MRTSx = MRTSy = w/r). When wage rates differ across sectors, the ratio of factor prices differs between industries: (wx/r) > (wy/r).\n- Consequently, the economy operates off the Edgeworth contract curve. Production takes place strictly INSIDE the original production possibility frontier (PPF), shrinking the attainable production set.\n\n3. Non-Tangency and Breakdown of the Gains-from-Trade Theorem:\n- The marginal rate of transformation (MRT) no longer equals the domestic price ratio (Px/Py).\n- At free trade prices, the value of production may not be maximized on the PPF. The country may specialize in the wrong direction or consume at a utility level lower than autarky (immiserizing trade distortion).\n- Policy implication: Free trade is not first-best when domestic factor market distortions exist; the first-best policy is a domestic tax-subsidy directly addressing the wage gap (the Principle of Targeting).",
