    "suppliedAnswer": "1. The Prebisch-Singer Thesis (1950):\n- Raul Prebisch and Hans Singer observed that the net barter terms of trade (Tot = Px/Pm) of primary producing developing countries have experienced a secular downward trend relative to manufactured goods from industrialized nations.\n- Reasons for Terms of Trade Decline:\n  (a) Asymmetry in Market Structure: Industrial countries have monopolistic product markets and powerful labor unions that capture productivity gains as higher wages and profits, keeping manufactured prices high. Developing countries have competitive commodity markets; productivity gains lower primary export prices.\n  (b) Low Income Elasticity of Demand (Engel’s Law): The global income elasticity of demand for food and raw materials is inelastic (ey < 1). As world income grows, demand for manufactures expands much faster than demand for agricultural and mineral exports.\n  (c) Synthetic Substitutes: Technological progress develops artificial substitutes (synthetic rubber, plastics, fibers) replacing natural primary imports.\n\n2. Ragnar Nurkse’s Export Pessimism:\n- Nurkse argued that the 19th-century trade engine, where primary exports stimulated rapid domestic growth, is no longer functional for 20th and 21st-century developing countries.\n- Heavy reliance on a single primary commodity (e.g. Nigerian petroleum) exposes the nation to external demand shocks, balance of payments crises, and Dutch Disease, justifying industrial diversification and inward structural transformation.",
